SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:--------------------------------------------------------------
 --\nOrientation Honorlock Practice and AI Agreement Quiz (Proctored)\n----
 ------------------------------------------------------------\n\nIn this ac
 tivity\, you will review the course AI policy and set up Honorlock for pro
 ctored exams. You will then complete a short practice quiz that includes a
  reflection on the AI policy.\n\nPurpose of the Quiz\n-------------------
 \n\nThis quiz serves two purposes: to help you become familiar with the on
 line proctoring process and to confirm your understanding of the course AI
  policy.\n\nBefore continuing in the course\, review the AI policy below\,
  complete the Honorlock setup\, and submit the quiz. Doing this now will h
 elp you prepare for your first proctored exam and avoid technical problems
  later.\n\nThe quiz includes a short reflection on the AI policy. Your res
 ponse will also provide an example of your natural writing style\, which m
 ay be helpful if an AI detector produces a false positive in the future. W
 rite in the same voice and style you would normally use for a paper in thi
 s course.\n\nRead the AI Policy\n------------------\n\nArtificial Intellig
 ence can be useful in a host of situations\, but not in writing assignment
 s and papers for this course. In IHUM 202\, we want you to develop critica
 l thinking skills as you work your way through ideas in your own head. We 
 want you to gain new insights and come to your own conclusions. Then we wa
 nt you to tell us about those conclusions in your own voice. We don't want
  you to delegate your thinking and writing to AI. This is why we beg you a
 nd plead with you to not use AI to write your assignments and papers. No
 t only is it academically dishonest to pass off AI-generated text as your 
 own\, but the AI bot that generates that text is bound to create a pretty 
 soulless product. You—yes\, human you—are blessed with a unique\, indi
 vidual mind and a unique\, individual voice. Believe in your ability to us
 e both. We genuinely want to hear what human you has to say.\n\nJust to cl
 arify\, the following are unacceptable uses of AI in this course:\n\n* Usi
 ng AI to generate the text of your assignment or paper (for example\, plug
 ging a prompt into ChatGPT and submitting what Chat GPT creates).\n\n* Usi
 ng AI to generate the text of your assignment or paper\, and then making a
  few changes so that there is a bit of your own writing somewhere in there
 .\n\n* Asking AI to revise your initial draft\, to fix sections of your pa
 per\, or to make your draft sound more college level\, more formal\, etc. 
 This goes beyond a mere spellcheck\, and substantially changes the wording
  and/or organization of your original writing. Using Grammarly's editing f
 eature would fall under this violation. \n\n* Using AI to generate ideas 
 or an outline for a paper or assignment that you then flesh out in your ow
 n writing.\n\nIf an assignment or paper is flagged by Turnitin or other AI
  detectors as having potentially over 70%  AI-generated text\, we'll be r
 eaching out to have a conversation with you about how you completed the as
 signment or paper. If we determine that AI was used to substantially gener
 ate or alter your writing\, the assignment or paper will receive a zero.\n
 \nIf an assignment or paper is flagged by Turnitin or other AI detectors a
 s having potentially between 50% and 70% of AI-generated text\, we'll be r
 eaching out to have a conversation with you about how you completed the as
 signment or paper. If we determine that AI was used to substantially gener
 ate or alter your writing\, the assignment or paper will receive a 50% ded
 uction. \n\nThis AI policy is based on the BYU Comparative Arts and Lette
 rs Position on AI. We recommend you familiarize yourself with it as well.
  \n\nBYU Comparative Arts and Letters Position on AI\n-------------------
 ----------------------------\n\nPractice\n--------\n\nAI and LLMs in the 
  BYU Comparative Arts & Letters Classroom\n\nYou won’t find the same AI 
 policies in a humanities class as what you may encounter in other fields a
 cross campus. Reading\, writing\, brainstorming\, editing\, visualizing\, 
 creating\, and analyzing texts and artworks are crucial enterprises that s
 hould not be offloaded without clear-eyed deliberation about their effects
  (in all realms - cognitive\, ethical\, social\, aesthetic\, spiritual\, e
 tc.). Pay attention to each of your instructor’s policies and be ready t
 o commit to humanistic work in its richness and rigor. As a peculiar peopl
 e\, we should not be afraid to go against the cultural grain--to go slower
  when the world demands we speed up\; to savor instead of consume\; to que
 stion\, rather than prompt\; to listen more deeply rather than scroll more
  voraciously\; to painstakingly craft rather than reproduce or simulate. W
 e understand the inseparable relationships of culture and technology\, mat
 eriality and media\, and believe that ongoing experience and spiritual dis
 cernment are vital to navigating those relationships in our teaching\, res
 earch\, and spiritual mission. As Latter-day Saint humanists we believe ed
 ucation is aimed at nothing less than building the kingdom for the glory o
 f God\, whose glory is not artificial\, but authentic and embodied loving 
 intelligence.  \n\n \n\nPrinciples\n----------\n\nOn the importance of 
 being human(ists)\n\nOur understanding of the humanities as Latter-day Sai
 nts recognizes embodied experience as the central purpose of our mortal jo
 urney. The humanities are an exploration of mortal experience: of the hist
 orical and cultural forces that shape us and confront us as human beings\,
  and which provoke innumerable forms of human expressions and responses ov
 er time and across space. These expressions and responses in their varied 
 forms are the humanist’s objects of study. In the humanities\, form\, co
 ntent\, and context are interconnected in the process of making meaning\, 
 in contrast to other disciplines which may regard objects primarily in ter
 ms of the content they convey or their perceived utility. In addition\, th
 e kinds of cognitive offloading and deskilling that these technologies app
 ear to foster run counter to many of the goals of a BYU education and to o
 ur shared mortal purpose of gaining experience and intelligence through li
 ved experience. \n\nOn “generative” technologies\n\nTechnologies that
  purport to generate language at unprecedented scale and speed obscure the
  practices that backstop those technologies (some of which are ethically a
 nd morally dubious)\; they also make the practice of writing (and writing-
 as-thinking) seem trivial and automatable. All of this is of great concern
  to us as Latter-Day Saint humanists. We view large language models (chatb
 ots\, LLMs\, “AI”)  not as neutral or transparent tools of informatio
 n\, but products of cultural and corporate ideologies that must be the obj
 ects of study rather than means of outsourcing creativity and analysis. An
 y use of AI in the humanities is necessarily an exercise in critical think
 ing\, not simply a means of transmission.\n\nAcademic Integrity\n---------
 ---------\n\nPlease carefully review the following information regarding a
 cademic integrity.\n\nBy accessing BYU Online academic exams\, you acknowl
 edge that you have read and understand the BYU Academic Honesty Policy. Fo
 r clarity\, we want to remind you that the BYU Academic Honesty Policy and
  all related policies apply to you when you take this test or quiz\, and w
 e want to check your recognition of this fact. You agree to uphold the pri
 nciples of academic honesty and integrity\, follow all exam rules and guid
 elines (including the use of proctoring services)\, and adhere to the cons
 equences of any violations as outlined in the BYU Academic Honesty Policy.
  Please note that you must agree to each requirement in this quiz to proce
 ed with this course\n\nHonorlock\n---------\n\n[Preparing for Your Exam] (
 https://byu.instructure.com/courses/10881/pages/exam-instructions?module_i
 tem_id=2232810)\n\nModule Item Completion (browser only)
