 30px\; font-size: 24px\; background-color: #6b1f3a\;"><strong>Read the AI 
 Policy</strong></h3>\n<p>Artificial Intelligence can be useful in a host o
 f situations\, but not in writing assignments and papers for this course. 
 In IHUM 202\, we want you to develop critical thinking skills as you work 
 your way through ideas in your own head. We want you to gain new insights 
 and come to your own conclusions. Then we want you to tell us about those 
 conclusions in your own voice. We don't want you to delegate your thinking
  and writing to AI. This is why we beg you and plead with you to<span>&nbs
 p\;</span><strong>not</strong><span>&nbsp\;</span>use AI to write your ass
 ignments and papers. Not only is it academically dishonest to pass off AI-
 generated text as your own\, but the AI bot that generates that text is bo
 und to create a pretty soulless product. You—yes\, human you—are bless
 ed with a unique\, individual mind and a unique\, individual voice. Believ
 e in your ability to use both. We genuinely want to hear what human you ha
 s to say.</p>\n<p>Just to clarify\, the following are unacceptable uses of
  AI in this course:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Using AI to generate the text of your a
 ssignment or paper (for example\, plugging a prompt into ChatGPT and submi
 tting what Chat GPT creates).</li>\n<li>Using AI to generate the text of y
 our assignment or paper\, and then making a few changes so that there is a
  bit of your own writing somewhere in there.</li>\n<li>Asking AI to revise
  your initial draft\, to fix sections of your paper\, or to make your draf
 t sound more college level\, more formal\, etc. This goes beyond a mere sp
 ellcheck\, and substantially changes the wording and/or organization of yo
 ur original writing. Using Grammarly's editing feature would fall under th
 is violation.&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>Using AI to generate ideas or an outline fo
 r a paper or assignment that you then flesh out in your own writing.</li>
 \n</ul>\n<p>If an assignment or paper is flagged by Turnitin or other AI d
 etectors as having potentially over 70%&nbsp\; AI-generated text\, we'll b
 e reaching out to have a conversation with you about how you completed the
  assignment or paper. If we determine that AI was used to substantially ge
 nerate or alter your writing\, the assignment or paper will receive a zero
 .</p>\n<p>If an assignment or paper is flagged by Turnitin or other AI det
 ectors as having potentially between 50% and 70% of AI-generated text\, we
 'll be reaching out to have a conversation with you about how you complete
 d the assignment or paper. If we determine that AI was used to substantial
 ly generate or alter your writing\, the assignment or paper will receive a
  50% deduction.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><span>This AI policy is based on the BYU Co
 mparative Arts and Letters Position on AI. We recommend you familiarize yo
 urself with it as well.&nbsp\;</span></p>\n<div class="dp-panels-wrapper d
 p-accordion-default">\n<div class="dp-panel-group">\n<h3 class="dp-panel-h
 eading">BYU Comparative Arts and Letters Position on AI</h3>\n<div class="
 dp-panel-content">\n<h4 class="dp-margin-direction-tblr cp-txt-dp-primary 
 dp-text-bold" style="margin-top: 20px\; font-size: 18px\; margin-bottom: 1
 0px\; color: #6b1f3a\;">Practice</h4>\n<hr class="dp-hr-solid-light dp-mar
 gin-direction-tblr" style="border-color: #eeeeee\; color: #c7c9c7\; margin
 -top: 8px\; margin-bottom: 20px\;">\n<p><span> <i>AI and LLMs in the&nbsp
 \; BYU Comparative Arts &amp\; Letters Classroom</i> </span></p>\n<p><span
 >You won’t find the same AI policies in a humanities class as what you m
 ay encounter in other fields across campus. Reading\, writing\, brainstorm
 ing\, editing\, visualizing\, creating\, and analyzing texts and artworks 
 are crucial enterprises that should not be offloaded without clear-eyed de
 liberation about their effects (in all realms - cognitive\, ethical\, soci
 al\, aesthetic\, spiritual\, etc.). Pay attention to each of your instruct
 or’s policies and be ready to commit to humanistic work in its richness 
 and rigor. As a peculiar people\, we should not be afraid to go against th
 e cultural grain--to go slower when the world demands we speed up\; to sav
 or instead of consume\; to question\, rather than prompt\; to listen more 
 deeply rather than scroll more voraciously\; to painstakingly craft rather
  than reproduce or simulate. We understand the inseparable relationships o
 f culture and technology\, materiality and media\, and believe that ongoin
 g experience and spiritual discernment are vital to navigating those relat
 ionships in our teaching\, research\, and spiritual mission. As Latter-day
  Saint humanists we believe education is aimed at nothing less than buildi
 ng the kingdom for the glory of God\, whose glory is not artificial\, but 
 authentic and embodied loving intelligence.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</span></p>\n<div
 ><span>&nbsp\;</span></div>\n<h4 class="dp-margin-direction-tblr cp-txt-dp
 -primary dp-text-bold" style="margin-top: 20px\; font-size: 18px\; margin-
 bottom: 10px\; color: #6b1f3a\;">Principles</h4>\n<hr class="dp-hr-solid-l
 ight dp-margin-direction-tblr" style="border-color: #eeeeee\; color: #c7c9
 c7\; margin-top: 8px\; margin-bottom: 20px\;">\n<p><span> <i>On the import
 ance of being human(ists)</i> </span></p>\n<p><span>Our understanding of t
 he humanities as Latter-day Saints recognizes embodied experience as the c
 entral purpose of our mortal journey. The humanities are an exploration of
  mortal experience: of the historical and cultural forces that shape us an
 d confront us as human beings\, and which provoke innumerable forms of hum
 an expressions and responses over time and across space. These expressions
  and responses in their varied forms are the humanist’s objects of study
 . In the humanities\, form\, content\, and context are interconnected in t
 he process of making meaning\, in contrast to other disciplines which may 
 regard objects primarily in terms of the content they convey or their perc
 eived utility. In addition\, the kinds of cognitive offloading and deskill
 ing that these technologies appear to foster run counter to many of the go
 als of a BYU education and to our shared mortal purpose of gaining experie
 nce and intelligence through lived experience.&nbsp\;</span></p>\n<p><span
 > <i>On “generative” technologies</i> </span></p>\n<p><span>Technologi
 es that purport to generate language at unprecedented scale and speed obsc
 ure the practices that backstop those technologies (some of which are ethi
 cally and morally dubious)\; they also make the practice of writing (and w
 riting-as-thinking) seem trivial and automatable. All of this is of great 
 concern to us as Latter-Day Saint humanists. We view large language models
  (chatbots\, LLMs\, “AI”)&nbsp\; not as neutral or transparent tools o
 f information\, but products of cultural and corporate ideologies that mus
 t be the objects of study rather than means of outsourcing creativity and 
 analysis. Any use of AI in the humanities is necessarily an exercise in cr
 itical thinking\, not simply a means of transmission.</span></p>\n</div>\n
 </div>\n</div>\n<div class="dp-callout dp-callout-placeholder card dp-call
